<!--
  Solution for the Linux measurement boxes (Stryker, fuzzing).

  THE FILENAME IS THE WARNING. Open PokemonBattleJournal.slnx in Visual Studio; this one has no
  app head and no UI test projects, so a VS session on it silently cannot build, run or debug the
  app. It exists for `dotnet stryker --solution` on a headless Linux box and nothing else.

  WHY A SECOND SOLUTION FILE
  Stryker builds the containing solution before mutating, and the main PokemonBattleJournal.slnx
  cannot build on Linux for two independent reasons:

    * UITests.Windows needs FrameworkReference Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App — NETSDK1073, and
      there is no fixing that off Windows.
    * The app head multi-targets net10.0-android, which needs the Android SDK — XA5300. The
      workload supplies the bindings, not the SDK itself.

  Neither project is mutated or tested here, so the answer is to leave them out rather than to
  install an Android SDK on a box that will never run an emulator.

  THE APP HEAD IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and this is the subtle part. Tests and IntegrationTests
  both ProjectReference it, so it still gets built — but as a REFERENCE, which resolves to its
  net10.0 TFM alone. A solution build would instead build every TFM it declares, including
  android, which is exactly the failure above. Listing it here would reintroduce the problem it
  is excluded to avoid.

  Keep in sync with PokemonBattleJournal.slnx when a net10.0 project is added.
-->
<Solution>
  <Project Path="PokemonBattleJournal.Core/PokemonBattleJournal.Core.csproj" />
  <Project Path="PokemonBattleJournal.Scraper/PokemonBattleJournal.Scraper.csproj" />
  <Project Path="PokemonBattleJournal.Tests/PokemonBattleJournal.Tests.csproj" />
  <Project Path="PokemonBattleJournal.IntegrationTests/PokemonBattleJournal.IntegrationTests.csproj" />
  <Project Path="PokemonBattleJournal.Fuzz/PokemonBattleJournal.Fuzz.csproj" />
</Solution>
